  "worked_solution": "The power series expansion of $\\frac{1}{1+x}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1+x}=1-x+{x}^{2}-⋯.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the above by ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+x}={x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}-⋯.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}-⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-⋯){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{5}-⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
